# Normalise line endings in the repository, so everyone gets the same bytes.
#
# `* text=auto` lets Git detect text vs binary, then the rules below pin the
# result. Without this, a Windows clone checks out `gradlew` with CRLF and the
# Gradle wrapper fails on Linux and macOS with "bad interpreter" or
# "Permission denied" - the single most common way a fresh clone of an Android
# repo fails to build.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The Gradle wrapper is a shell script. It must be LF and must be executable,
# or `./gradlew` is not runnable.
gradlew text eol=lf
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar binary

# Build scripts and Kotlin sources: LF, and text.
*.kt text eol=lf
*.kts text eol=lf
*.java text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.pro text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf

# Windows batch files genuinely need CRLF to run.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never touched: these are not text, and normalising them corrupts them.
*.jar binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.9.png binary

# Note: `.gitattributes` has no negation syntax. `.env` needs no rule here -
# it is matched by `* text=auto eol=lf`, which is what we want for it. If it
# ever stops being tracked the cause will be a global `core.excludesFile`, not
# this file.
